<details>
<summary>LLVM IR — Dominance applies to allocation pointers too</summary>

Demoting arithmetic removes direct cross-block data uses, but the alloca result is itself an SSA pointer. If a dispatcher reaches a use without passing through that allocation, the IR is still invalid. Preserving entry storage and rejecting non-entry allocations are separate pieces of the design.

Checkpoint: Why does putting values in memory not eliminate every SSA dominance requirement?

</details>

<details>
<summary>LLVM IR — Why special call semantics constrain CFG rewriting</summary>

An ordinary call is not the whole story. musttail requires strict call/return structure; inserting a spill reload or transition between the call and return can violate its contract. returns_twice marks calls that can return more than once, so an apparently single forward path does not capture their control effects. A convergent operation has additional constraints on how control flow may be transformed around participating operations. Operand bundles carry semantic information beyond ordinary operands, such as deoptimization or funclet context.

These features are not inherently untransformable. This small pass lacks a feature-specific proof and implementation, so preflight rejects them before editing. In contrast, a normal recursive scalar call can remain in place if its result is prepared correctly. “Do not modify calls” is not enough: moving surrounding control flow or inserting value transport can still change a call contract.

Checkpoint: Why can a musttail call become invalid even when its opcode and callee are unchanged?

See the [LLVM call instruction reference](https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#call-instruction) and the specific attributes/bundles involved before relaxing a rejection.

</details>

