22. Orchestrate policy, preparation, and construction
=====================================================

Task: Future exercise: replace the prototype’s inline construction with the strict guard, preparation stage, and StateMachineBuilder call.

### The template to start with

```cpp
// 1. Skip declarations.
// 2. Check strict eligibility; reject without mutation.
// 3. Skip entry-only/no-successor functions.
// 4. Collect and demote PHIs.
// 5. Collect and demote suitable cross-block values.
// 6. Build the state machine.
// 7. Return the preservation set for all changes.
```

### Step 1 — Keep rejection before modification

No demotion or state allocation should precede the decision. Rejection after a partial transformation violates the unchanged-input contract.

### Step 2 — Combine change information without skipping work

```cpp
bool changed = !phis.empty() || !escaping.empty();
changed |= StateMachineBuilder::build(F);
```

Here |= is boolean accumulation that evaluates the builder call even if changed was already true. Writing changed = changed || build(F) would short-circuit and skip construction whenever demotion changed the function. Alternatively call build first into a separate bool, then combine the results.

### Step 3 — Preserve conservatively

Return none() after any mutation in this teaching implementation. Preserving a particular analysis is a claim you must justify and, for dependent cached analyses, implement correctly. “The graph still looks similar” is not enough.

### The full pass reference

```cpp
#include "minishield/FlatteningPass.h"
#include "minishield/FunctionEligibility.h"
#include "minishield/StateMachineBuilder.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Instructions.h"
#include "llvm/Support/Casting.h"
#include "llvm/Support/raw_ostream.h"
#include "llvm/Transforms/Utils/Local.h"
#include <vector>
namespace minishield {
llvm::PreservedAnalyses FlatteningPass::run(llvm::Function &F,
                                           llvm::FunctionAnalysisManager &) {
    if (F.isDeclaration()) return llvm::PreservedAnalyses::all();
    const auto decision = FunctionEligibility::check(F);
    if (!decision.eligible) {
        llvm::errs() << "[MiniShield] rejected: " << F.getName() << ": " << decision.reason << "\n";
        return llvm::PreservedAnalyses::all();
    }
    if (F.size() < 2 || F.getEntryBlock().getTerminator()->getNumSuccessors() == 0)
        return llvm::PreservedAnalyses::all();
    std::vector<llvm::PHINode *> phis;
    for (auto &BB : F)
        for (auto &I : BB)
            if (auto *phi = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::PHINode>(&I)) phis.push_back(phi);
    for (auto *phi : phis) llvm::DemotePHIToStack(phi);
    std::vector<llvm::Instruction *> escaping;
    for (auto &BB : F)
        for (auto &I : BB)
            if (!I.getType()->isVoidTy() && !llvm::isa<llvm::AllocaInst>(I) &&
                I.isUsedOutsideOfBlock(&BB)) escaping.push_back(&I);
    for (auto *I : escaping) llvm::DemoteRegToStack(*I);
    bool changed = !phis.empty() || !escaping.empty();
    changed |= StateMachineBuilder::build(F);
    return changed ? llvm::PreservedAnalyses::none() : llvm::PreservedAnalyses::all();
}
}
```

### Verify

Run stats before and after flattening in the same pipeline so cached analysis invalidation is exercised. Expect the second stats pass to observe the new blocks and edges, not the original cached CFG.

```bash
opt -load-pass-plugin="$PWD/build/libMiniShield.so" \
  -passes='function(minishield-stats,minishield-flatten,minishield-stats),verify' \
  -disable-output tests/ir/loop.ll
```

<details>
<summary>C++ — Short-circuiting can silently skip a transformation</summary>

```cpp
bool alreadyChanged = true;
// Bad orchestration: RHS is not evaluated.
alreadyChanged = alreadyChanged || build(F);
// Clear alternative:
bool built = build(F);
alreadyChanged = alreadyChanged || built;
```

This is C++ evaluation behavior, not an LLVM pass-manager feature. A change flag should summarize work, not accidentally control whether required work runs.

Checkpoint: How would you test that the builder runs when a PHI was already demoted?

</details>

Checkpoint: Which helper owns policy? Which phase changes data flow? Which creates CFG edges? What exactly does the final bool summarize?

### Check your understanding

Why can changed = changed || build(F) be wrong?

A. It may not call build when changed is already true
B. It always calls build twice
C. It turns a bool into an LLVM i1

Answer: A. C++ || short-circuits. Use a separate result or an accumulation form that still evaluates the builder.

